  "text": "{\n  \"cedar\": \"cobalt-lantern-8136\",\n  \"harbor\": \"silver-meadow-4729\",\n  \"maple\": \"violet-compass-9264\"\n}\n\n**How to distinguish authoritative audit records from ordinary event records**\n\nThe archive mixes two kinds of content. Ordinary records follow a highly regular template: an event identifier, a short hex-like code, a verb (validated / replayed / committed / rotated / released / retried / stored / checked), a partition, a revision, and boilerplate about a retained transaction receipt. Thousands of near-identical lines follow this pattern, and their content is inferable as routine operational noise. The authoritative entries are different in kind: they are `AUDIT_SECRET` lines that name a record key and a value, and they are not generated by the ordinary event grammar.\n\n**How an operator should distinguish them**\n\n- **Look for the marker, not the volume.** The three `AUDIT_SECRET` lines are the only lines that assign a key to a value. Everything else is an `Event` line. A parser should treat `AUDIT_SECRET <key>",
